<h2>The application</h2>
<p>The petitioner, about fifty years old and in custody since 14 May 2026, sought regular bail before Justice Anubha Rawat Choudhary in connection with Bagodar P.S. Case No. 98 of 2026, pending before the Special Judge, POCSO, at Giridih. The charges span Sections 74, 75 and 76 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and Sections 8 and 12 of the POCSO Act. His counsel pressed false implication and the trial&rsquo;s pace: cognizance and custody dated to mid-2026, charge was framed on 18 September 2026, yet no witness had been examined. The State opposed, pointing out that the informant is an eyewitness, that the child&rsquo;s statement under Section 183 BNSS supports the allegation, and that witnesses would be produced on the next date, 7 December 2026.</p>

<h2>Why custody continued</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Direct allegation.</strong> The accusation is specific to the petitioner, not an omnibus implication.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Eyewitness informant.</strong> The complaint does not rest on hearsay; the informant claims to have seen the occurrence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Unexamined child witness.</strong> The central deposition is pending — and the petitioner lives in the complainant family&rsquo;s neighbourhood, making the risk of influence concrete rather than theoretical.</div>
</div>

<h2>The counterweight: the State is put on terms</h2>
<p>What makes the order instructive is its second half. The Court did not simply refuse bail and move on; it converted the defence&rsquo;s delay grievance into enforceable directions. The State must produce its witnesses promptly — the order singles out the 7 December 2026 date — and must adhere to the POCSO Act&rsquo;s timelines. The Public Prosecutor is to communicate the order to the Superintendent of Police of the district and the Director of Prosecution, placing responsibility where witness production actually happens. And the renewal liberty is calibrated: if witnesses are still not produced despite the order, the petitioner may return in March 2027 — a renewal tied to the prosecution&rsquo;s default, not to the mere passage of time.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>The older pattern</strong><p>Bail refused in grave offences with liberty &ldquo;to renew after some witnesses are examined&rdquo; — leaving the accused hostage to a timetable nobody enforces.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>This order&rsquo;s pattern</strong><p>Bail refused, but witness production is directed, responsibility is assigned to named officials, and the renewal date is fixed against the State&rsquo;s own performance.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Reading it with the POCSO timelines</h2>
<p>The POCSO Act builds speed into its design: Section 35(1) requires the child&rsquo;s evidence to be recorded within thirty days of cognizance, with reasons for any delay; Section 35(2) contemplates trial completion within a year as far as possible. In practice, listing pressures and vacations erode both — the Court here noted the vacation that intervened after charge framing. Orders of this type restore the statute&rsquo;s balance: the accused&rsquo;s liberty interest and the child&rsquo;s interest in a swift, untainted deposition point in the same direction, towards an early trial date rather than an indefinite adjournment culture. For the defence, the practical course after such an order is disciplined patience: attend every date, create a clean record, and let the State&rsquo;s compliance — or default — write the renewed application.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information only, withholding all victim-identifying details. Nothing here comments on the merits of the pending trial.</div>
